      "id": 1,
      "text": "Early settler Thomas Riddick platted a town called 'Suffolk' on a Revolutionary War land grant, but it failed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rochester%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Rochester was named for Rochester, New York, reflecting settlers' ties to the larger city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rochester%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rochester sits at the confluence of the Green and Mud rivers, a strategic spot called 'The Mouth' in early pioneer days.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rochester%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city was established in the 1830s and incorporated in 1839, marking its formal recognition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rochester%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rochester Dam, located on the Green River just west of town, serves as a key flood‑control structure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
